WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.360
Hello, I'm Brian Cox.

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:04.240
I'm Robert Ince, and this is the Infinite Monkey Cage.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.760
Now, all of you, and I imagine there's been a few daydreamers here,

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.240
when you stare out of the window at school, that's what they say.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.760
Concentrate! What are you doing staring out of the window?

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:16.040
Well, for some people, it wasn't just daydreaming.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.400
It was practising to become a meteorologist or a cloud expert.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.120
Or an astronomer, I suppose.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.559
Or an astronomer. What time did you go to school?

00:00:23.559 --> 00:00:25.080
LAUGHTER

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.639
I'm from Oldham.

00:00:26.639 --> 00:00:29.280
Oh, what? From Oldham, the sky, we're at...

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.160
It's always dark, dark, with industrial plumes.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.439
You've not bought your clogs again, have you?

00:00:35.439 --> 00:00:37.919
Cos they made a right racket last time.

00:00:37.919 --> 00:00:41.639
I couldn't wear them standing on those volcanoes looking enigmatic,

00:00:41.639 --> 00:00:44.040
could I? Cos the clicking would spoil the...

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:48.480
Oh, they're not made for going over overcooled lava, are they?

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:49.959
No. Oh, OK.

00:00:49.959 --> 00:00:52.680
They're made of wood. It's the worst possible thing to wear,

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.959
to be honest, walking through lava, isn't it?

00:00:54.959 --> 00:00:57.919
Isn't it? There must be something... Well, paper, I suppose.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.560
LAUGHTER

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:03.040
Oh, no. Oh, cos we only had paper shoes in Oldham. Yeah, whatever.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.879
Anyway, today we're looking to the sky,

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:06.599
but not as far as Brian likes to look.

00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:09.839
It's a kind of parochial level of looking to the sky.

00:01:09.839 --> 00:01:12.000
We're going to go, well, really, the level of clouds.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.839
The clouds that, of course, so often hindered your series' stargazing.

00:01:15.839 --> 00:01:19.239
Why were there always clouds there? Was it Dara O Briain's head?

00:01:19.239 --> 00:01:21.480
Because I think because of the roundness of the head,

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.199
they might have attracted clouds closer.

00:01:23.199 --> 00:01:24.680
LAUGHTER

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.519
Today we're asking, how do clouds help

00:01:26.559 --> 00:01:29.160
in maintaining... I can't say maintaining.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:30.919
I'll do it if you want. Here we go. You do it.

00:01:30.919 --> 00:01:36.199
Today we're asking, how do clouds help in maintaining a healthy atmosphere?

00:01:36.199 --> 00:01:39.160
What can clouds, which are so beautiful and so wonderful,

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.760
and sometimes they shine with ice crystals,

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.000
what can they teach us about the climate?

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.760
Now you've got to do my line, doing me.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.959
LAUGHTER

00:01:47.959 --> 00:01:49.160
To guide us through!

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:52.519
LAUGHTER

00:01:52.519 --> 00:01:54.000
That's Bernard Mann, isn't it?

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.320
To guide us through!

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:56.559
Oh, you can tell that.

00:01:56.559 --> 00:01:58.839
To guide us through, we're joined by a climate scientist.

00:01:58.839 --> 00:02:01.239
Don't worry, it's all right. The show's going to be OK.

00:02:01.239 --> 00:02:02.480
LAUGHTER

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.800
To guide us through, we're joined by a climate scientist,

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:07.839
a cloud appreciator and, well, a drummer.

00:02:07.839 --> 00:02:10.240
Oh, but so much more than a drummer.

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:11.240
And they are.

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.160
My name's Gavin Predepinney, I'm member number one

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.279
and the founder of the Cloud Appreciation Society,

00:02:17.279 --> 00:02:19.559
which I started 20 years ago,

00:02:19.559 --> 00:02:22.479
and it brings people together from all around the world,

00:02:22.479 --> 00:02:26.880
120 countries we've got members in, all united in the belief that,

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.479
well, clouds aren't something to complain about,

00:02:29.479 --> 00:02:32.320
they're actually one of the most evocative and dynamic

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:36.199
and maybe you could say poetic aspects of nature.

00:02:36.199 --> 00:02:41.639
And the most wonderful thing I've seen in a morning sky

00:02:41.639 --> 00:02:43.279
is a morning glory.

00:02:43.279 --> 00:02:44.440
LAUGHTER

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:46.720
Which you'll be relieved...

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:47.720
No!

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.000
LAUGHTER

00:02:49.039 --> 00:02:53.759
It is a cloud formation that forms in a remote part

00:02:53.759 --> 00:02:56.000
of northern Queensland in Australia.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:59.600
It's a cloud that glider pilots surf,

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.199
just like surfers on an ocean wave.

00:03:02.199 --> 00:03:05.119
It travels across the Gulf of Carpentaria through the night

00:03:05.119 --> 00:03:10.759
and it arrives first thing in the morning on the Queensland coast.

00:03:10.759 --> 00:03:13.639
And I went there and I watched this cloud arrive,

00:03:13.639 --> 00:03:16.960
I rushed up into a plane with all the other gliders

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.639
and watched them as they surfed up and down this cloud

00:03:19.639 --> 00:03:21.800
as the sunrise was breaking over the top of it.

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.399
It was an absolutely glorious and beautiful sight.

00:03:24.399 --> 00:03:26.240
Hello, I'm Amanda Maycock.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.759
I'm an atmospheric scientist and a professor from the University of Leeds.

00:03:30.759 --> 00:03:33.960
I use computer models to simulate the movement of the air

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.039
within the Earth's atmosphere, including clouds,

00:03:36.039 --> 00:03:39.080
and how it affects the Earth's climate.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:43.800
The most wonderful thing I saw in this morning's sky,

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.119
there weren't many clouds around up in Leeds today,

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:49.639
was actually Venus, which is visible at the moment.

00:03:49.639 --> 00:03:53.000
And Venus is another beautiful cloudy planet,

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.039
but a bit less hospitable than Earth

00:03:55.039 --> 00:03:57.399
because the clouds are made of sulfuric acid

00:03:57.399 --> 00:03:59.960
and they've contributed to a runaway greenhouse effect

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.240
that means the surface temperature is more than 450 degrees Celsius.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.080
So that was the beautiful thing I saw this morning.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:09.520
Don't worry, you'll have those sulfuric acids before you know it.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.199
They're working really hard in the US and also...

00:04:12.199 --> 00:04:14.520
LAUGHTER

00:04:14.559 --> 00:04:17.040
I'm Owein Wyn Evans. I used to be a weather presenter.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.799
Now I am a TV and radio presenter and I'm also a drummer.

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:23.760
I got the name the Drumming Weatherman

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.239
when I played the drums to the BBC News music in lockdown.

00:04:27.239 --> 00:04:32.440
It went viral and then I went on to play the drums for 24 hours

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.679
for a Children in Need drum-a-thon.

00:04:35.679 --> 00:04:38.480
Whilst every so often also doing the weather.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:40.119
LAUGHTER

00:04:40.119 --> 00:04:43.679
My husband was thrilled to have the drum kit out of the house for 24 hours.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:48.079
I was thrilled to throw the drum kit into a skip after the drum-a-thon.

00:04:48.079 --> 00:04:52.440
And the most wonderful thing that I've seen in a morning sky

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:56.600
is a gigantic pepper pig balloon.

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:58.119
Stay with me on this one.

00:04:58.119 --> 00:05:02.920
I live in Penarth, which is near the water in South Wales.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:04.959
And me and Aaron were going for a walk one morning

00:05:04.959 --> 00:05:07.480
and saw this kind of orb over the water.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:09.480
I thought, Aaron, what's that?

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.399
Aaron, that looks like pepper pig.

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:14.839
It was a daddy pig actually.

00:05:14.839 --> 00:05:18.000
As the balloon approached it was indeed pepper pig.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.279
It was drifting up the coast towards Swansea.

00:05:20.279 --> 00:05:23.799
Stranger things have drifted up the coast towards Swansea.

00:05:23.799 --> 00:05:26.040
It turned out to be a child's balloon

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:32.760
which was somehow amplified and expanded by the view across the sea.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.640
Pepper pig drifted towards us and off she went.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:39.160
Last seen off the coast of Barry.

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.839
As I think a lot of hopes and dreams have.

00:05:41.839 --> 00:05:44.160
So there we go.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:46.160
And this is our panel.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:53.040
Gavin, we should start with you.

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:56.799
Why did you feel a cloud appreciation society was so important?

00:05:56.799 --> 00:05:59.920
It's because people do like to complain about them a bit.

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:03.760
You don't need like a sort of kitten appreciation society

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.079
because everyone loves them.

00:06:05.119 --> 00:06:09.040
You need something that's a society that has a kind of mission

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:12.359
to shift people's perspective about the sky.

00:06:12.359 --> 00:06:13.720
At least that's what I felt.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:18.519
So really I just felt someone needed to stand up for clouds basically.

00:06:18.519 --> 00:06:20.760
Let's get this out of the way. What's your favourite formation?

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:22.399
It could be a pileus.

00:06:22.399 --> 00:06:24.399
Fair enough, that's a very strong answer.

00:06:24.399 --> 00:06:27.399
LAUGHTER

00:06:27.399 --> 00:06:31.440
Amanda, we need to move back to the science.

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.760
That's not science!

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:35.799
Move back to the science.

00:06:35.799 --> 00:06:39.600
We had a man talking about a pepper pig balloon moment ago.

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:41.720
We've got to bring him gently back to the science.

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:44.679
I'm just saying, what's your favourite cloud?

00:06:44.679 --> 00:06:47.320
I said favourite formation, Brian.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:49.320
Is there a technical difference between the...

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.320
Have I made a terrible mistake?

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:53.640
Formation is just a general term

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:56.000
because basically they're all clouds.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.000
They're these random appearances in the sky.

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:01.399
But humans are amazing at spotting patterns.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:06.279
And so when we started to classify clouds in a scientific way,

00:07:06.279 --> 00:07:08.519
1802, around then,

00:07:08.519 --> 00:07:12.160
and it was actually an Englishman called Luke Howard,

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:14.440
a Quaker from Tottenham,

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:18.600
he was spotting patterns and he started to give them Latin names.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:23.079
So he came up with the cumulus and cirrus and stratus.

00:07:23.079 --> 00:07:25.839
And these names just basically say what the clouds look like.

00:07:25.839 --> 00:07:29.480
And you say it in Latin, sounds fancy, makes it sound scientific.

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:33.320
See, Brian, you put too much effort in learning physics.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:39.720
Amanda, how do clouds, at the most basic level, how do clouds form?

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:44.000
So a cloud forms when a parcel of air becomes saturated

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:47.440
and some of the moisture that it contains condenses.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:49.480
So where does that come from, the moisture in the air?

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:53.519
Well, if you imagine standing and drawing a sort of square around your feet

00:07:53.519 --> 00:07:54.640
that was sort of one metre squared

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.040
and you weighed all of the water above your head,

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:00.079
there's on average about 25 kilograms of water

00:08:00.079 --> 00:08:01.640
all the way up to the top of the atmosphere,

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:02.959
which doesn't sound like a lot,

00:08:02.959 --> 00:08:04.920
it's certainly a lot less than the rest of the mass of the air,

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:07.519
but it is enough to form these clouds.

00:08:07.519 --> 00:08:10.079
So basically the evaporation mainly comes from over the oceans,

00:08:10.079 --> 00:08:12.760
we've got the vast oceans that provide that source.

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:14.959
And the sun heats the surface of the earth,

00:08:14.959 --> 00:08:17.160
the air warms up, warm air rises,

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:18.480
it moves up through the atmosphere

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:20.839
and as it does so it cools and it expands.

00:08:20.839 --> 00:08:22.200
And it's that expansion and cooling

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.559
that can cause the air to become saturated

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:28.279
and that's the point at which the cloud droplets can begin to form.

00:08:28.279 --> 00:08:29.679
But they don't just form by themselves,

00:08:29.679 --> 00:08:31.839
they need something to cling on to, as it were,

00:08:31.839 --> 00:08:34.919
and they tend to form around little things you can't see,

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:37.039
specks in the air, they might be bits of dust,

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:39.840
bits of pollen, fungal spores from trees

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:43.799
and the water molecules condense around those little particles

00:08:43.799 --> 00:08:45.479
and start to grow a droplet.

00:08:45.479 --> 00:08:47.280
And then where does the structure come from?

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.119
Because we've heard that there are,

00:08:49.119 --> 00:08:52.440
how many, is there a number for how many different types

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:54.719
of classified cloud formations?

00:08:54.719 --> 00:08:57.080
Yeah, well, there are 10 basic types,

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:59.960
the genera, the main types,

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:03.559
and those are the ones you'll have heard of at school,

00:09:03.559 --> 00:09:07.080
cumulus cirrostratus, cumulonimbus,

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:09.960
the superstar, you know, the storm cloud

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.799
that produces thunder and lightning and hail.

00:09:12.799 --> 00:09:16.320
But then there are lots of these additional ones

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.719
which are sometimes just a feature

00:09:18.719 --> 00:09:22.080
that looks just like a curl, like a tiny breaking wave,

00:09:22.719 --> 00:09:25.559
which would be called a fluctus cloud feature.

00:09:25.559 --> 00:09:26.799
There's a whole bunch of them.

00:09:26.799 --> 00:09:28.840
I think there's about, when you combine them all,

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:31.479
it's probably about 80, something like that.

00:09:31.479 --> 00:09:32.400
And one of them actually came

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.280
from the Cloud Appreciation Society.

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:35.119
Oh, yeah.

00:09:35.119 --> 00:09:36.280
Yeah, one of the official types.

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:38.520
So what is giving the clouds

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:42.119
these different characteristics, broadly speaking?

00:09:42.119 --> 00:09:45.559
So there's two main sort of types of particle

00:09:45.559 --> 00:09:46.640
that we can find within clouds.

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:47.919
There's liquid water droplets

00:09:47.919 --> 00:09:49.559
and then there are ice crystals.

00:09:49.559 --> 00:09:50.679
And so a lot of the appearance

00:09:50.719 --> 00:09:51.760
and the differences in appearance

00:09:51.760 --> 00:09:54.599
come from how much liquid water they contain

00:09:54.599 --> 00:09:56.840
or how much ice crystals they contain.

00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:00.320
So the high altitude ice clouds, where it's very cold,

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:01.840
they have this kind of wispy appearance.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:03.960
They get sort of stretched out by the winds.

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:05.799
So the winds are obviously blowing the clouds around.

00:10:05.799 --> 00:10:07.359
You can often see that on a windy day.

00:10:07.359 --> 00:10:09.119
You'll see them being blown along.

00:10:09.119 --> 00:10:10.719
The lower altitude clouds,

00:10:10.719 --> 00:10:12.599
which are made from liquid water,

00:10:12.599 --> 00:10:14.440
the sort of fluffy cumulus type clouds,

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:15.520
they're sort of formed

00:10:15.520 --> 00:10:18.080
because of the liquid water within them.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:19.359
So, Owen, what about for you?

00:10:19.359 --> 00:10:21.320
I know technically you'd be called a weather forecaster,

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:23.320
but you weren't actually forecasting weather, was you?

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:24.640
Yeah, that's right, yeah.

00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:27.760
I came from a background of journalism.

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:30.799
When I joined BBC Weather,

00:10:30.799 --> 00:10:34.960
the Met Office were providing the sort of data, if you like.

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:37.400
So I was trained up by them

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:40.719
and then later studied through the Open University,

00:10:40.719 --> 00:10:43.039
meteorology through a couple of modules,

00:10:43.039 --> 00:10:48.039
just because I think we love the weather here in the UK.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:51.919
It's the first point of conversation for a lot of people.

00:10:51.919 --> 00:10:53.000
If you don't know what to say,

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.599
it's lovely outside, it's terrible, isn't it?

00:10:55.599 --> 00:11:00.039
Not always that camp, by the way, terrible weather.

00:11:00.039 --> 00:11:03.280
But I think that for me,

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:06.200
the clouds are the things that a lot of people,

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:07.679
it's that thing that you can see.

00:11:07.679 --> 00:11:10.479
And I remember when I was doing the weather in lockdown,

00:11:10.479 --> 00:11:11.960
there was nothing to talk about

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:13.359
because people weren't really going out.

00:11:13.359 --> 00:11:15.719
So the producers would give me three minutes

00:11:15.719 --> 00:11:16.559
to fill on the weather.

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:19.039
Hans, everyone's inside.

00:11:19.039 --> 00:11:22.039
But people would then start to see,

00:11:22.039 --> 00:11:23.280
like you were saying earlier, Gavin,

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:27.599
about the clouds, you start to see shapes or faces.

00:11:27.599 --> 00:11:30.239
And I think that's what's amazing about clouds.

00:11:30.239 --> 00:11:33.080
It almost gives people a little bit of creativity,

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:37.000
especially cumulus clouds, the fluffy cotton wool ones.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:38.359
What about, Gavin was saying about

00:11:38.359 --> 00:11:41.440
that there's a negative attitude towards clouds.

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:43.799
I imagine when you were mainly doing the kind of,

00:11:43.799 --> 00:11:44.719
the presenting the weather,

00:11:44.719 --> 00:11:46.640
you would get letters of complaint

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:49.400
as if you yourself had created the weather.

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:51.159
I mean, did you get that?

00:11:51.159 --> 00:11:52.440
Oh, I still do.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:54.400
I've under the weather for three years

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:56.719
and people still say, oh, didn't forecast this, did you?

00:11:56.719 --> 00:11:59.200
So, well, hon, if I forecast this three years ago,

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:02.479
I'd be monetizing this skill somehow.

00:12:02.479 --> 00:12:04.280
It would happen a lot when we get,

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:05.919
we would get bad weather as well.

00:12:05.919 --> 00:12:07.919
You know, if there were really bad storms

00:12:07.919 --> 00:12:10.320
and we'd get weather warnings,

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:12.080
the yellow warning is the lowest,

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:13.559
then it goes up to amber warning,

00:12:13.559 --> 00:12:15.599
which is my drug name as well.

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:20.239
I do think where the presenters are a bit too apologetic,

00:12:20.239 --> 00:12:22.200
they sort of seem to feel that

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:26.159
and they feel a bit guilty when they say, you know,

00:12:26.159 --> 00:12:28.559
there's going to be a lot of cloud around

00:12:28.559 --> 00:12:30.880
in an apologetic tone.

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:32.880
I would like to hear a bit more detail

00:12:32.880 --> 00:12:35.239
about what cloud is going to be around.

00:12:35.239 --> 00:12:38.799
I'd like to know how the formations

00:12:38.799 --> 00:12:41.080
are going to be changing through the afternoon.

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:43.799
I'd like to know what sort of shapes we're going to be seeing.

00:12:43.799 --> 00:12:47.039
I would like some more detail and a bit more positivity.

00:12:47.039 --> 00:12:49.000
I'd love that. I'd love that.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:50.039
We could do like a kind of a,

00:12:50.039 --> 00:12:52.200
what shapes will we see today in the clouds?

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:54.239
Like a little bet, maybe.

00:12:54.239 --> 00:12:56.080
Did you used to send to the,

00:12:56.080 --> 00:12:58.359
because they only used to have one shape of magnet

00:12:58.359 --> 00:12:59.599
for the cloud, didn't they?

00:12:59.599 --> 00:13:01.320
Whatever, whether it was a stormy cloud, whatever.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:02.799
It was sort of a bit of a sad day

00:13:02.799 --> 00:13:04.719
when they stopped using those magnets.

00:13:04.719 --> 00:13:07.200
But I mean, that is the cumulus cloud,

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:08.640
the one that's in the cartoon.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:11.719
And it's like the sort of generic cloud, the cumulus.

00:13:11.719 --> 00:13:14.119
If you close your eyes and think of a cloud,

00:13:14.119 --> 00:13:16.200
it's one of these puffy white cumuluses

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:18.440
that form on a sunny day.

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:20.799
They feel the most solid.

00:13:20.799 --> 00:13:25.479
The word cloud comes from the old English word clod,

00:13:25.479 --> 00:13:27.799
which means a rock or a boulder.

00:13:27.799 --> 00:13:30.799
It's where we get the clod of earth, that term from.

00:13:30.799 --> 00:13:33.679
These feel like solid things, cumulus clouds.

00:13:33.679 --> 00:13:34.520
Do you know what I mean?

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:36.440
And that's why I think they're kind of iconic for us.

00:13:36.479 --> 00:13:38.919
They have very, very distinctive shapes

00:13:38.919 --> 00:13:41.679
and the edge of them is very well-defined,

00:13:41.679 --> 00:13:43.400
which seems counterintuitive.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:46.239
It's just this phenomenon where water is condensing.

00:13:46.239 --> 00:13:49.400
So why do they have such a well-defined shape?

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:50.479
So it's often because of,

00:13:50.479 --> 00:13:53.159
there's a sort of barrier of the air to be mixed

00:13:53.159 --> 00:13:55.239
from within the cloud to the surrounding cloud.

00:13:55.239 --> 00:13:56.599
So there's differences in temperature,

00:13:56.599 --> 00:13:59.239
differences in density between different air masses

00:13:59.239 --> 00:14:00.719
that are nearby each other.

00:14:00.719 --> 00:14:02.799
So that's what provides this kind of

00:14:02.799 --> 00:14:04.919
rather crisp looking shape often.

00:14:04.919 --> 00:14:07.919
The base of the cloud will be where the kind of

00:14:07.919 --> 00:14:10.200
layer of the atmosphere starts to become saturated.

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:11.640
I mentioned before the saturation

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:12.960
is a sort of necessary condition.

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:15.479
So again, that's something that will have

00:14:15.479 --> 00:14:17.599
a sort of well-defined base to it.

00:14:17.599 --> 00:14:19.159
And then at the top of the cloud,

00:14:19.159 --> 00:14:21.280
you'll move into drier air, warmer air above,

00:14:21.280 --> 00:14:23.960
but they're not sort of stationary things.

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:26.039
What's actually happening in reality

00:14:26.039 --> 00:14:27.799
is that there's constant evaporation

00:14:27.799 --> 00:14:30.799
and condensation occurring at the base of the cloud.

00:14:30.799 --> 00:14:32.039
So the base of the cloud,

00:14:32.080 --> 00:14:35.479
the little droplets will be continuously evaporating.

00:14:35.479 --> 00:14:36.520
And then at the top of the cloud,

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:38.039
there'll be condensing and reforming.

00:14:38.039 --> 00:14:40.239
So they're very sort of dynamic structures.

00:14:40.239 --> 00:14:41.520
So is that, because I sometimes,

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:43.520
I do find it fascinating when you're looking

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:46.400
on a day where the wind is moving a cloud

00:14:46.400 --> 00:14:47.479
and you look at that cloud and you think

00:14:47.479 --> 00:14:49.640
it has remained in that shape and yet,

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:52.799
so actually within what appears to be

00:14:52.799 --> 00:14:56.080
that kind of the stationary nature of the shape itself,

00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:59.119
there is still all manner of movement going on there.

00:14:59.119 --> 00:14:59.960
Yeah, absolutely.

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:02.840
There's a lot of motion that's happening within the clouds.

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:03.919
In fact, that's one of the reasons

00:15:03.919 --> 00:15:06.200
why a cloud doesn't just fall out of the sky.

00:15:06.200 --> 00:15:07.559
So a typical cumulus cloud,

00:15:07.559 --> 00:15:09.640
the ones we're talking about might weigh

00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:12.159
somewhere between about 250 or 400 tons,

00:15:12.159 --> 00:15:14.880
something like a sort of typical 747 aircraft,

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:15.719
something like that.

00:15:15.719 --> 00:15:16.559
There's a lovely gasp over there.

00:15:16.559 --> 00:15:19.679
People are slightly more scared of clouds than they used to be.

00:15:19.679 --> 00:15:20.520
What if they fall?

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:21.359
What if they fall?

00:15:21.359 --> 00:15:22.400
Chicken licking?

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:23.400
How big would that be?

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:27.080
So a 400 ton cumulus cloud, what dimensions?

00:15:27.080 --> 00:15:29.359
So that would be of the order of a sort of cubic kilometer

00:15:29.359 --> 00:15:32.239
or something like that, one and a half kilometers cubic.

00:15:32.239 --> 00:15:35.479
That's a sort of typical size of a cumulus cloud.

00:15:35.479 --> 00:15:37.119
But of course the gravity isn't acting

00:15:37.119 --> 00:15:38.039
on the cloud as a whole.

00:15:38.039 --> 00:15:40.200
It's acting on the individual tiny little droplets,

00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:41.919
which are of the order of few thousandth

00:15:41.919 --> 00:15:44.440
of a millimeter across within the cloud.

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:46.400
And so actually they only fall very, very slowly,

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:49.159
perhaps a centimeter per second or something like that.

00:15:49.159 --> 00:15:50.840
And you actually have motion within the cloud,

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:54.080
upward motion, which sort of basically sustains

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:55.640
the altitude of those droplets.

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:58.159
So they don't just drop out and fall down.

00:15:58.159 --> 00:16:00.520
They collide and coalesce with each other.

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:02.359
They join up and become bigger droplets.

00:16:02.359 --> 00:16:03.520
And eventually once they're heavy enough,

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:06.479
they can actually fall out and become precipitation.

00:16:06.479 --> 00:16:09.520
This is the magic of clouds,

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:14.520
comes from the special nature of water

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:18.080
that's naturally found in the three states

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:19.919
of solid, liquid and gas.

00:16:19.919 --> 00:16:24.679
And that is really the secret of this kind of poetic

00:16:24.679 --> 00:16:26.280
and beautiful quality of clouds

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:29.599
because they can appear and disappear at will

00:16:29.599 --> 00:16:33.000
because one of those states, the gas one is invisible.

00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:36.799
And so we're at the triple point of these three states

00:16:36.799 --> 00:16:39.719
on earth in the atmosphere of earth.

00:16:39.719 --> 00:16:44.400
And so the clouds are revealing the changes,

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:47.000
revealing the invisible movements of the air.

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:50.039
These movements of the winds are happening the whole time.

00:16:50.039 --> 00:16:54.320
We only see them when they just nudge that water

00:16:54.359 --> 00:16:57.440
to shift state from the gas form we can't see

00:16:57.440 --> 00:16:59.520
to the liquid or the solid.

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:01.479
That's a very beautiful thought actually

00:17:01.479 --> 00:17:03.799
is that they make the atmosphere visible.

00:17:03.799 --> 00:17:05.400
They're the face of the atmosphere.

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:08.400
They're expressions on the face of the atmosphere.

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:10.439
And so they can reveal the moods,

00:17:10.439 --> 00:17:14.199
the shifting, ever shifting moods of the atmosphere.

00:17:14.199 --> 00:17:18.160
And we just intrinsically know how to read those moods.

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:21.680
Ask a child when they see a cloud that is dark

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:23.520
with a base that's low

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:25.520
and they know what's going to happen.

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:28.599
They know that that's very likely to produce rain.

00:17:28.599 --> 00:17:30.719
You know, we've always known that right back to,

00:17:30.719 --> 00:17:33.400
I imagine, you know, when we were living in caves.

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:34.640
Now we know the science of it.

00:17:34.640 --> 00:17:36.040
We know why,

00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:39.400
but they've always been this shifting expression

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:41.000
on the atmosphere that, you know,

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:42.959
I think we need to be more in tune with.

00:17:42.959 --> 00:17:44.880
I think it is good for the mental health

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:48.839
and your well-being to be engaged with the sky personally.

00:17:48.839 --> 00:17:49.680
What is it?

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:50.520
What's the physics of that?

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:53.319
So why are rain clouds dark?

00:17:54.119 --> 00:17:55.880
First of all, I mean, clouds look white,

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:57.800
most of them, liquid clouds look white

00:17:57.800 --> 00:18:00.599
because they're scattering the incoming radiation

00:18:00.599 --> 00:18:01.680
that's coming from the sun.

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:03.640
And they scatter that radiation quite equally

00:18:03.640 --> 00:18:05.400
across different wavelengths of light.

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:07.640
And that's why they look white.

00:18:07.640 --> 00:18:09.479
Often if you see a kind of gray cloud,

00:18:09.479 --> 00:18:11.280
it's sometimes a shadowing effect.

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:12.880
So it's kind of the cloud's own shadow.

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:15.199
So if it's a very large kind of low cloud, as you said,

00:18:15.199 --> 00:18:16.760
it's basically kind of shadowing

00:18:16.760 --> 00:18:19.760
and you can't see that scattering effect as well.

00:18:19.760 --> 00:18:21.400
How bright they look is sort of dependent

00:18:21.400 --> 00:18:22.319
on how they're made.

00:18:22.319 --> 00:18:24.959
So if you have lots of small little droplets

00:18:24.959 --> 00:18:25.800
within the cloud,

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:27.920
then it is very good at scattering that light.

00:18:27.920 --> 00:18:30.199
It will look very bright and very white.

00:18:30.199 --> 00:18:33.079
As the droplets grow and become larger,

00:18:33.079 --> 00:18:36.079
then it sort of becomes less effective at the scattering.

00:18:36.079 --> 00:18:38.199
And it's the tallness of the cloud

00:18:38.199 --> 00:18:41.439
that's super important when it comes to a dark base

00:18:41.439 --> 00:18:45.280
because a cloud that's tall has a dark base

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:48.359
because it just, the light doesn't really get through.

00:18:48.359 --> 00:18:51.439
And a cloud that's tall is much more likely

00:18:51.439 --> 00:18:53.680
to produce precipitation

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:55.959
because it often starts with the droplets

00:18:55.959 --> 00:18:57.920
starting to freeze at the top.

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:00.239
It starts as snow, melts on the way down,

00:19:00.239 --> 00:19:02.119
maybe lands as rain,

00:19:02.119 --> 00:19:06.959
but that tallness is crucial to a lot of precipitation.

00:19:08.000 --> 00:19:10.839
One of the things that I love about clouds as well,

00:19:10.839 --> 00:19:13.560
you know, you mentioned there about how they appear white

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:17.319
because they scatter the wavelengths of color,

00:19:17.319 --> 00:19:19.280
but Rayleigh scattering,

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:21.640
which is what makes the sky blue

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:26.239
because the sky isn't physically blue

00:19:26.239 --> 00:19:28.000
because at nighttime you can see through it

00:19:28.000 --> 00:19:29.319
up to the stars, right?

00:19:29.319 --> 00:19:30.319
And then in the morning,

00:19:30.319 --> 00:19:33.079
when the sun is lower in the sky

00:19:33.079 --> 00:19:36.880
and the light is sort of grazing the atmosphere of the earth,

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:39.160
those wavelengths are scattered again

00:19:39.160 --> 00:19:41.719
and you get more of the reds coming through

00:19:41.719 --> 00:19:44.000
because the red wavelength is kind of,

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:47.319
could we describe it a bit like a wibbly wobbly wave?

