A wibbly wobbly fashion.

00:23:26.160 --> 00:23:27.719
He thought he was being,

00:23:27.719 --> 00:23:29.880
I think he just thought he was being Bob Morton,

00:23:29.920 --> 00:23:32.280
but it's actually a really good physics question,

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:33.119
isn't it?

00:23:33.119 --> 00:23:35.239
Because as you said, I didn't think of that actually,

00:23:35.239 --> 00:23:37.280
the radiation back out again.

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:39.199
Is that one of the reasons you feel turbulence

00:23:39.199 --> 00:23:41.800
when you enter clouds in an aircraft?

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:43.520
Yeah, well, that's exactly what you're experiencing.

00:23:43.520 --> 00:23:46.000
All of those upward motions I described before

00:23:46.000 --> 00:23:46.839
that are happening,

00:23:46.839 --> 00:23:48.239
all of the local currents that are happening

00:23:48.239 --> 00:23:49.959
within the cloud is basically what causes

00:23:49.959 --> 00:23:52.199
that turbulence for the aircraft.

00:23:52.199 --> 00:23:53.760
The top of the cloud in particular,

00:23:53.760 --> 00:23:55.479
because you've basically got a body

00:23:55.479 --> 00:23:56.800
which is emitting radiation,

00:23:56.800 --> 00:23:58.599
emitting thermal radiation,

00:23:58.599 --> 00:24:00.920
and that's dependent on the temperature

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:02.160
at the top of the cloud, basically.

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:04.199
So how cold the top of the cloud is determines

00:24:04.199 --> 00:24:05.839
how much energy it's going to emit out.

00:24:05.839 --> 00:24:09.040
Is it always hotter at a higher temperature

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:11.760
than the air, the clear air above it?

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:15.359
Depends on if it's an unstable atmosphere,

00:24:15.359 --> 00:24:17.719
because in a stable atmosphere,

00:24:17.719 --> 00:24:20.599
a thermal might rise from the ground and come back down

00:24:20.599 --> 00:24:23.040
because it's not warmer than the surrounding atmosphere.

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:27.400
And you don't have clouds forming or storms developing,

00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:31.359
but in an unstable atmosphere, as we describe it,

00:24:31.359 --> 00:24:33.920
the air gets more rapidly cold as you go up.

00:24:33.920 --> 00:24:35.640
And therefore, if you lift some air

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:37.959
from near the ground where it's warm,

00:24:37.959 --> 00:24:41.280
it comes up, some droplets begin to form as it cools.

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:43.880
And remember, every time a tiny droplet forms,

00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:46.040
a little bit of heat is released,

00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:49.000
the latent heat as it changes state.

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:50.319
And those all add up,

00:24:50.319 --> 00:24:53.160
even though it's a tiny amount from each tiny droplet.

00:24:53.160 --> 00:24:56.760
And this is like fuel to keep the buoyancy

00:24:56.800 --> 00:24:58.280
in an unstable atmosphere.

00:24:58.280 --> 00:25:00.719
So the droplets form, it gets more buoyant,

00:25:00.719 --> 00:25:02.520
more droplets form, it gets more buoyant,

00:25:02.520 --> 00:25:04.079
and you get building, building, building

00:25:04.079 --> 00:25:07.000
to this enormous cathedral of clouds

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.760
that is the cumulonimbus storm cloud.

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:12.359
And within that, these currents,

00:25:12.359 --> 00:25:16.359
vertical currents are rising rapidly in some areas,

00:25:16.359 --> 00:25:19.719
precipitation's falling fast in other areas,

00:25:19.719 --> 00:25:22.760
and sometimes hailstones develop

00:25:22.760 --> 00:25:26.040
because these particles are going round and round

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:27.680
and building up in layers of ice.

00:25:27.680 --> 00:25:29.319
So Bob Mortimer's crisp packet

00:25:29.319 --> 00:25:32.760
could have ended up in the middle of a hailstone.

00:25:32.760 --> 00:25:35.560
It's gonna be one of those very famous physics examples

00:25:35.560 --> 00:25:38.520
that all undergraduates learn in atmospheric physics,

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:39.359
isn't it?

00:25:39.359 --> 00:25:40.199
Mortimer's crisp packet.

00:25:40.199 --> 00:25:44.160
That was the infinite monkey cage, you know?

00:25:45.160 --> 00:25:47.479
But just to pick up on that point,

00:25:47.479 --> 00:25:50.000
because it's an interesting bit of physics,

00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:54.319
the fact that water droplets forming release energy.

00:25:54.319 --> 00:25:57.199
Could you just maybe, Amanda, just speak about that?

00:25:57.199 --> 00:26:00.319
Yeah, so going back to Gavin's triple point for water,

00:26:00.319 --> 00:26:02.719
so any time that water changes phase,

00:26:02.719 --> 00:26:05.040
so whether it goes from a gas to a liquid

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:07.239
or from a liquid to a solid,

00:26:07.239 --> 00:26:08.319
in that direction, it's, yeah,

00:26:08.319 --> 00:26:10.040
basically releasing a little bit of energy,

00:26:10.040 --> 00:26:11.439
which is to do with the organisation

00:26:11.439 --> 00:26:13.719
of the water molecules, basically, within the structure.

00:26:13.719 --> 00:26:16.520
So once it condenses and it turns to a liquid,

00:26:16.520 --> 00:26:18.000
that little bit of energy gets released.

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:18.839
And as Gavin said,

00:26:18.839 --> 00:26:21.040
that contributes to a heating in the atmosphere.

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:23.400
It's basically this latent heating effect of the clouds

00:26:23.400 --> 00:26:24.880
as they're forming.

00:26:24.880 --> 00:26:26.000
And you get the opposite as well.

00:26:26.000 --> 00:26:29.040
So you could have ice crystals up at the top

00:26:29.040 --> 00:26:30.920
of one of these cumulonimbus clouds.

00:26:30.920 --> 00:26:32.439
They spread out in an anvil,

00:26:32.439 --> 00:26:35.160
this enormous plume of ice crystals.

00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:37.880
It spreads out hundreds and hundreds of miles.

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:42.239
And in one area, those ice crystals can melt

00:26:42.239 --> 00:26:44.920
and therefore change state in the other way.

00:26:44.920 --> 00:26:46.839
That makes the air cool a bit.

00:26:46.839 --> 00:26:50.119
And you can get these sinking pockets

00:26:50.119 --> 00:26:53.839
that appear as mama clouds on the underside,

00:26:53.839 --> 00:26:57.000
these pouches of cloud on the underside

00:26:57.000 --> 00:27:01.560
of one of these cumulonimbus anvil plumes.

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:03.160
And they look really distinctive,

00:27:03.160 --> 00:27:04.160
these pouches of clouds.

00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:06.119
And they're the result of the cooling,

00:27:06.119 --> 00:27:08.280
most likely, it's not totally understandable,

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:10.479
most likely the result of the cooling that happens

00:27:10.479 --> 00:27:12.920
with the shift in state the other way.

00:27:12.920 --> 00:27:15.400
We've all seen those, haven't we, those anvil clouds.

00:27:15.400 --> 00:27:18.680
Why are they so flat at the top of the cloud?

00:27:18.680 --> 00:27:20.520
So going back to what Gavin talked about

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:22.439
with the stability of the atmosphere.

00:27:22.439 --> 00:27:24.760
So particularly in the tropics,

00:27:24.760 --> 00:27:26.920
we find a lot of these deep cumulonimbus clouds

00:27:26.920 --> 00:27:28.400
over the warm tropical oceans.

00:27:28.400 --> 00:27:30.119
There's a huge amount of fuel and moisture

00:27:30.119 --> 00:27:31.599
which allows these clouds to get up

00:27:31.599 --> 00:27:34.599
to altitudes of 18 or 19 kilometers.

00:27:34.599 --> 00:27:36.239
This is sort of almost twice the height

00:27:36.239 --> 00:27:38.319
of cruise altitudes of typical aircraft.

00:27:38.319 --> 00:27:40.560
So these are really tall clouds.

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:42.479
And what happens is you hit a sort of boundary,

00:27:42.479 --> 00:27:43.839
we call it the tropopause.

00:27:43.839 --> 00:27:45.119
It's basically the transition

00:27:45.119 --> 00:27:46.640
between the lower part of the atmosphere

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:48.839
where all of our weather happens and the clouds form

00:27:48.839 --> 00:27:50.839
and the layer above it, which is called the stratosphere.

00:27:50.839 --> 00:27:53.400
And it's called stratosphere because it's stratified layer.

00:27:53.400 --> 00:27:54.760
It's a stable layer.

00:27:54.760 --> 00:27:56.520
And so the clouds basically get up to this,

00:27:56.520 --> 00:27:57.760
it's almost like a lid.

00:27:57.760 --> 00:28:00.560
They can't move above that because the air above is stable.

00:28:00.560 --> 00:28:01.920
And so they can only spread out.

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:03.479
And that's why you get these anvil clouds

00:28:03.479 --> 00:28:04.959
because they then just spread sideways

00:28:04.959 --> 00:28:07.040
because they can't travel further upwards anymore.

00:28:07.040 --> 00:28:10.239
And if you see the photographs from the space station

00:28:10.239 --> 00:28:13.359
where one of the astronauts takes a photo down

00:28:13.359 --> 00:28:15.239
onto cumulonimbus clouds,

00:28:15.280 --> 00:28:19.079
it's like they've formed underneath a piece of glass.

00:28:19.079 --> 00:28:21.520
There's this temperature profile,

00:28:21.520 --> 00:28:24.040
this temperature inversion, we call it,

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:25.479
because it's the opposite of the normal

00:28:25.479 --> 00:28:26.640
getting colder as you go up.

00:28:26.640 --> 00:28:30.040
It starts to get warmer at the top of the troposphere.

00:28:30.040 --> 00:28:32.199
And that's this invisible lid

00:28:32.199 --> 00:28:34.839
and the cloud you can just see spread out.

00:28:34.839 --> 00:28:36.880
And it just can't go up any, it wants to go up higher,

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:37.920
but it can't go up any higher

00:28:37.920 --> 00:28:39.520
because it suddenly gets stable there.

00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:42.040
This is another incredible thing about clouds

00:28:42.040 --> 00:28:45.479
is how you get so many different kind of shapes

00:28:45.479 --> 00:28:48.560
and reasons as why things look a certain way

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:49.680
or are a certain way.

00:28:49.680 --> 00:28:53.239
And they've intrigued people forever.

00:28:53.239 --> 00:28:56.719
And I suppose it's only fairly recently

00:28:56.719 --> 00:29:01.719
that we as people have kind of fully been able to understand

00:29:01.719 --> 00:29:04.239
what these things are and why they're doing those things.

00:29:04.239 --> 00:29:05.719
I was going to ask that question actually

00:29:05.719 --> 00:29:09.239
about the, in terms of the physics of the atmosphere,

00:29:09.239 --> 00:29:11.640
what's the history of the scientific understanding

00:29:11.640 --> 00:29:12.800
of the physics of the atmosphere?

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:16.400
How recently did we begin to get a good picture

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:17.239
of what's happening?

00:29:17.239 --> 00:29:20.920
In terms of the modern understanding of the atmosphere.

00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:23.040
So in the sort of 17, 1800s,

00:29:23.040 --> 00:29:26.000
when people were getting into sort of ballooning

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:28.439
in a big way and starting to try

00:29:28.439 --> 00:29:30.640
and leave the surface of the earth

00:29:30.640 --> 00:29:32.680
and with them were taking some instruments

00:29:32.680 --> 00:29:34.800
to sort of measure temperature and pressure

00:29:34.800 --> 00:29:35.680
and trying to really understand

00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:37.280
what the vertical structure of the atmosphere is.

00:29:37.280 --> 00:29:39.599
That was really the first time that we started to get

00:29:39.599 --> 00:29:40.800
some of this basic information

00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:43.040
about temperature decreasing with height

00:29:43.040 --> 00:29:44.079
as you go through the troposphere,

00:29:44.079 --> 00:29:46.199
pressure decreasing with height.

00:29:46.199 --> 00:29:48.520
So that was a sort of, those were really key foundations.

00:29:48.520 --> 00:29:50.680
I think in the more modern era,

00:29:51.920 --> 00:29:53.560
the advances in technologies we've had,

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:58.160
the ability to measure through things like radars, lidars,

00:29:58.160 --> 00:30:00.640
through satellite measurements with radiometers,

00:30:00.640 --> 00:30:03.439
that's really given us a much more global view.

00:30:03.439 --> 00:30:05.479
Aircraft measurements, flying through clouds

00:30:05.479 --> 00:30:07.640
and actually measuring these droplets.

00:30:07.640 --> 00:30:08.479
How big are they?

00:30:08.479 --> 00:30:10.079
How many of them are there?

00:30:10.079 --> 00:30:11.359
What's sitting in the nucleus

00:30:11.359 --> 00:30:12.719
of those little droplets and things?

00:30:12.719 --> 00:30:13.760
All of those technologies

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:16.040
in the last sort of 50 or 60 years probably

00:30:16.040 --> 00:30:18.400
has really revolutionized our understanding.

