We've talked about weather,

00:34:23.919 --> 00:34:26.439
but can we now talk about climate?

00:34:26.479 --> 00:34:31.040
What role do the clouds play in climate modelling?

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:33.879
So the clouds have a really important effect

00:34:33.879 --> 00:34:36.159
on the balance of energy of our planet.

00:34:36.159 --> 00:34:38.159
So they do two main things.

00:34:38.159 --> 00:34:41.600
We talked earlier on about the scattering by clouds

00:34:41.600 --> 00:34:44.080
of the incoming radiation from the sun.

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:45.919
So that has the effect of cooling the planet.

00:34:45.919 --> 00:34:47.639
So less of that sun,

00:34:47.639 --> 00:34:49.479
solar radiation reaches the surface of the earth

00:34:49.479 --> 00:34:50.320
and causes heating.

00:34:50.320 --> 00:34:51.399
So that's a cooling effect,

00:34:51.399 --> 00:34:53.879
but they also can contribute to warming,

00:34:53.879 --> 00:34:55.840
particularly the higher altitude clouds,

00:34:55.840 --> 00:34:57.159
which are colder.

00:34:57.159 --> 00:34:58.879
We talked about the clouds themselves

00:34:58.879 --> 00:35:01.800
emitting their own radiation, their own thermal energy,

00:35:01.800 --> 00:35:04.000
and they therefore have a sort of trapping effect

00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:06.040
and can contribute to heating of the planet.

00:35:06.040 --> 00:35:08.360
So the overall effect that they have on the climate

00:35:08.360 --> 00:35:10.760
is the balance between two opposing effects,

00:35:10.760 --> 00:35:13.000
this cooling effect and the warming effect.

00:35:13.000 --> 00:35:14.280
Now in the current day,

00:35:14.280 --> 00:35:15.840
the net of those two things

00:35:15.840 --> 00:35:18.800
is that the clouds contribute to a cooling of the climate.

00:35:18.800 --> 00:35:20.560
So if we did a sort of thought experiment

00:35:20.560 --> 00:35:22.399
and got rid of all the clouds on the planet

00:35:22.399 --> 00:35:25.120
and thought about what that would mean for our climate,

00:35:25.120 --> 00:35:26.439
roughly we'd expect the temperature

00:35:26.439 --> 00:35:28.600
to be about 10 degrees warmer at the surface

00:35:28.600 --> 00:35:29.520
than without the clouds.

00:35:29.520 --> 00:35:30.360
So it's quite a significant effect.

00:35:30.360 --> 00:35:31.679
So the average global temperature.

00:35:31.679 --> 00:35:33.080
The average temperature, yes,

00:35:33.080 --> 00:35:34.560
would be significantly warmer

00:35:34.560 --> 00:35:36.800
if we didn't have that cooling effect.

00:35:36.800 --> 00:35:39.879
So they're absolutely critical for the climate.

00:35:39.879 --> 00:35:41.439
A really important question is, of course,

00:35:41.439 --> 00:35:44.760
what's happening to clouds now that the planet is warming

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:47.199
and the atmosphere is becoming warmer.

00:35:47.199 --> 00:35:48.600
And so the big question is,

00:35:48.600 --> 00:35:51.280
which of those two competing effects will win out

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:53.800
in terms of whether the cloud changes

00:35:53.800 --> 00:35:56.639
might amplify the warming of the planet

00:35:56.639 --> 00:35:58.719
that's occurring due to greenhouse gas increases

00:35:58.719 --> 00:36:00.919
or whether they might offset it.

00:36:00.919 --> 00:36:04.159
So at the moment, basically the science evidence as it is,

00:36:04.159 --> 00:36:08.080
we think that the clouds will contribute to further warming.

00:36:08.080 --> 00:36:10.320
So they'll basically amplify the global warming

00:36:10.320 --> 00:36:12.600
that's coming from the greenhouse gas increases

00:36:12.600 --> 00:36:13.439
in the atmosphere.

00:36:13.439 --> 00:36:16.320
And it's a sort of positive feedback effect in that way.

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:20.120
Clouds drive climate scientists crazy, don't they?

00:36:20.120 --> 00:36:23.959
Because they mess up their computer models.

00:36:23.959 --> 00:36:28.919
They're so dynamic and the system is so complex

00:36:28.919 --> 00:36:31.760
that you only need a slight change in the clouds

00:36:31.760 --> 00:36:34.360
and then fast forward your model 50 years.

00:36:34.360 --> 00:36:36.360
And it's entirely different

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:38.360
from if the clouds did something else.

00:36:38.360 --> 00:36:41.080
So you've got this really complex role

00:36:41.080 --> 00:36:44.360
that the clouds play in temperatures down in the ground.

00:36:44.360 --> 00:36:46.479
High ones have a warming effect.

00:36:46.479 --> 00:36:49.159
Low ones have a cooling effect.

00:36:49.159 --> 00:36:54.159
And then this change in clouds that seems to be emerging,

00:36:55.120 --> 00:36:57.600
you know, we've had studies look at how clouds have changed

00:36:57.600 --> 00:37:01.439
over 24 years of consistent satellite data.

00:37:01.439 --> 00:37:06.439
And you start to see fewer of those low cooling clouds

00:37:06.919 --> 00:37:10.000
in the cloudier regions of our planet.

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:12.800
And these are sort of worrying signs

00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:15.520
that the clouds may be shifting

00:37:15.520 --> 00:37:17.879
and that shift may have a feedback loop.

00:37:17.879 --> 00:37:19.120
What of the uncertainties,

00:37:19.120 --> 00:37:22.080
the major uncertainties in climate models?

00:37:22.080 --> 00:37:23.360
Our understanding of cloud,

00:37:23.360 --> 00:37:25.399
is that one of the bigger problems

00:37:25.399 --> 00:37:26.600
that we have in the models?

00:37:26.600 --> 00:37:28.520
It's the biggest source of uncertainty

00:37:28.520 --> 00:37:30.320
that we have as climate scientists

00:37:30.320 --> 00:37:32.280
in being able to kind of quantify

00:37:32.280 --> 00:37:34.439
how much warmer the planet might be

00:37:34.439 --> 00:37:36.840
in sort of 50 years from now, for example.

00:37:36.840 --> 00:37:38.479
So we're very, very confident

00:37:38.479 --> 00:37:41.280
that as greenhouse gases increase in the atmosphere,

00:37:41.280 --> 00:37:42.479
the planet will warm.

00:37:42.479 --> 00:37:44.879
But exactly by how much is more difficult

00:37:44.879 --> 00:37:47.520
because of these feedback loops that start to kick in

00:37:48.159 --> 00:37:50.479
as the atmosphere is warming and clouds are really tricky.

00:37:50.479 --> 00:37:52.840
As Gavin said, we need to use computer models

00:37:52.840 --> 00:37:56.479
to make projections and predictions into the future.

00:37:56.479 --> 00:37:58.959
And so we're solving lots of complicated equations

00:37:58.959 --> 00:38:01.199
on big supercomputers,

00:38:01.199 --> 00:38:03.479
but the scales of the clouds, as we've been discussing,

00:38:03.479 --> 00:38:05.280
are absolutely microscopic.

00:38:05.280 --> 00:38:06.959
You're talking about droplets, you know,

00:38:06.959 --> 00:38:10.040
of the size of a few thousandth of a millimetre,

00:38:10.040 --> 00:38:11.719
you know, and you need to then represent that

00:38:11.719 --> 00:38:13.120
at a global scale.

00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:14.239
So it's a real challenge.

00:38:14.239 --> 00:38:15.959
And what we need to do in our models

00:38:15.959 --> 00:38:18.959
is kind of use physics to enable us

00:38:18.959 --> 00:38:20.879
to represent those small scale processes

00:38:20.879 --> 00:38:22.399
across a larger area.

00:38:22.399 --> 00:38:24.080
We're in a really exciting time now, I think,

00:38:24.080 --> 00:38:26.840
where we're able to have enough computer power

00:38:26.840 --> 00:38:29.080
where we can actually start to resolve

00:38:29.080 --> 00:38:31.000
finer and finer spatial scales

00:38:31.000 --> 00:38:33.639
and we can actually run computer models, you know,

00:38:33.639 --> 00:38:36.320
with scales of the orders of tens of metres

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:39.120
rather than, you know, hundreds of kilometres.

00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:40.280
And at those scales,

00:38:40.280 --> 00:38:42.600
you're really then explicitly representing

00:38:42.600 --> 00:38:44.560
all of that physics in the model,

00:38:44.600 --> 00:38:46.600
the microphysics and the interactions

00:38:46.600 --> 00:38:49.479
of the droplets with the little particles and so on

00:38:49.479 --> 00:38:51.600
is all being simulated by the model.

00:38:51.600 --> 00:38:52.760
And you start to see really interesting things

00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:55.120
happening then, like convective organisation, for example,

00:38:55.120 --> 00:38:56.879
where clouds, little pockets of cloud

00:38:56.879 --> 00:38:59.159
actually through their own circulations

00:38:59.159 --> 00:39:01.600
will draw together and become a larger cloud system

00:39:01.600 --> 00:39:04.639
and turn into a mesoscale convective system, for example,

00:39:04.639 --> 00:39:07.159
one of these bigger storm clouds by themselves.

